  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1235 PM CDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nA 594 dm upper ridge is centered over the Southern Plains while a\n1024 mb surface high is centered near the Mid-Atlantic coast. As\na result, light southerly winds are advecting higher dewpoints,\nin the lower 70s, into areas east of the Mississippi River.\nDewpoints are still fairly low west of the Mississippi River,\nmainly in the upper 50s and lower 60s. The upper ridge will\ncontinue to expand over the Mid-South this afternoon with highs\nin the mid to upper 90s. The increased humidity will result in\nsome triple-digit heat index values mainly east of the MS River\nthis afternoon.\n\nSoutherly winds will spread lower to perhaps mid 70s dewpoints\nover the entire Mid-South on Monday. As the upper ridge continues\nto strengthen, expect the highest temperatures over western\nsections of the Mid-South. The humidity combined with\ntemperatures in the mid to upper 90s will result in heat index\nvalues of 105F or more across east Arkansas, the Missouri\nBootheel, northwest Mississippi, and portions of West Tennessee.\nA Heat Advisory is in effect from noon to 8 pm on Monday for\nthose aforementioned areas.\n\nHeat headlines will likely expand across the entire Mid-South as\nthe upper ridge persists through the week and the heat and\nhumidity builds. Highs in the mid to upper 90s will continue into\nnext weekend along with humid conditions. A few locations will\nprobably hit the low 100s across the Delta.\n\nA weak disturbance moving westward through the Deep South may\nresult in a few isolated showers of thunderstorms late in the\nweek into next weekend. There is some indication that the ridge\nmay retrograde west by late next weekend as a trough develops\nover the eastern CONUS. This may push a back door cold front\nthrough with slightly lower temperatures and lower humidities.",
